# Make English the only canonical base language

English is mandatory base-game input. Other official original-game languages
are preserved and supported, but exported as SHAR mods rather than compiled
into the canonical base package.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Define and validate the exact English source identities required by the
  faithful base pipeline.
- [x] Treat English as required, not optional and not merely a minimum-count
  language bucket.
- [x] Export every supported non-English official language as a deterministic,
  inspectable SHAR mod without losing original content.
- [x] Keep base gameplay/content identity independent from optional language
  mods and make language-mod composition deterministic.
- [x] Update README installation, validation, modding, and troubleshooting text
  when the implementation lands.

## Dependencies

Coordinate with source validation and normalized mod-package contracts.

## Evidence

The minimum ledger now owns exact mandatory English/base identities instead of
letting alternate-language counts substitute for English: `README.rtf`,
`dialog.rcf`, the English `srr2.E` character-set sidecar, and the multilingual
`srr2.txt` source table that carries the canonical `E`/`ENGLISH` column. The
original game source independently identifies `dialog.rcf` as English and the
`dialogf/g/i/s.rcf` files as alternate-language dialogue archives.

French, German, Italian, and Spanish TextBible sidecars and dialogue archives
are represented as exact optional identities (`min: 0`), as are the localized
readmes observed in the installation. They no longer contribute generic
language minimum buckets. Root runtime DLL/INI/save-state files are excluded by
narrow source rules so they cannot become accidental base requirements.

The canonical manifest validator now runs as the first extraction-stage gate,
closing the previous exact-header bypass. `cargo test -p game_manifest` and the
794-test pipeline library suite pass, and the local `game/` passes
`validate-game` with 103 curated generic minimums plus exact required sources.
Canonical language-mod generation now lives in the internal Rust crate
`src/localization/languages/`, not as a user-facing tool. The contract covers the
original TextBible bytes/sidecar, dialogue RCF, localized readme, localized
loading/license UI assets, and normalized cinematic audio. Original runtime FMV indexes 1/2/3
map to extracted tracks 02/03/04 for French/German/Spanish. Rust tests prove
French full-surface composition, Spanish track selection, and fail-closed UI
requirements. A read-only probe of the current lawful source finds 21 localized
loading/license assets for each of French/German/Spanish, dialogue archives of
166,793,216 / 186,839,040 / 156,291,072 bytes respectively, and eight normalized
movie packages carrying each localized track 02/03/04. English track 01 exists
for all 16 normalized movie packages. Earlier disposable probes exported French,
German, and Spanish bundles with 1,632 text records each and then cleaned all
489 MiB of probe output. French/German each retain one authored `???` value;
Spanish has none.

The same real probe correctly rejected Italian: that installation's entire
Italian column is `???` and `dialogi.rcf` is absent. The Rust domain keeps the
Italian language identity for installations that actually contain translated
source, but will not invent usable text, dialogue, UI, or cinematic audio from
placeholders or absent files.

`9079045c` introduced the shared storage-independent `shar.mod-package.v1`
contract with canonical identity, deterministic content revision, explicit
priority/conflicts, exact member lengths and SHA-256 identities, provenance,
portable NFC path validation, and the content/native trust boundary. `3e194a40`
then adapted official-language generation to that same contract: every published
language bundle carries `mod.json`, `shar.localization.<language>` identity,
content-only trust, exact package members, deterministic revision, and explicit
conflicts with the other official language overlays. Repeated synthetic exports
are byte-identical and the Rust suite now has 10 passing language tests including
package-contract parsing and complete Spanish dialogue/text/cinematic membership.

The README now states English as the sole canonical base language, explains the
normalized official-language package shape in validation/mod installation text,
and documents fail-closed troubleshooting for unavailable localization. No new
real-game probe was claimed on the Fedora workstation: the lawful-source counts
and French/German/Spanish full-surface evidence above remain the recorded real
source evidence, while current synthetic tests prove the final package adapter.
